# Literature Review
## Satellite Image Super-Resolution

### 1. Problem Background

Satellite images may have limited spatial resolution, which can
make small structures and fine details difficult to observe.
Super-resolution aims to reconstruct a high-resolution image from
a low-resolution input.

### 2. Traditional Methods

Interpolation methods such as nearest-neighbor, bilinear and
bicubic interpolation can increase image dimensions. However,
they do not learn complex image features and may produce blurred
details.

### 3. CNN-Based Super-Resolution

Convolutional neural networks learn feature representations from
low-resolution images and use these features to reconstruct
high-resolution images.

### 4. GAN-Based Super-Resolution

Generative Adversarial Networks use a generator and discriminator.
The generator attempts to reconstruct realistic high-resolution
images, while the discriminator distinguishes generated images
from real images.

### 5. Real-ESRGAN

Real-ESRGAN is a practical image restoration and super-resolution
approach. In this project, it will be investigated as a pretrained
baseline for satellite image super-resolution.

### 6. Transformer-Based Super-Resolution

Transformer-based architectures can model relationships between
different regions of an image and capture long-range dependencies.

### 7. Swin Transformer

Swin Transformer uses hierarchical representations and shifted
windows to efficiently model visual information.

### 8. Proposed Research Direction

The project will investigate a Transformer/GAN-based approach for
satellite image super-resolution. The proposed approach will be
compared with a traditional bicubic baseline and a pretrained
Real-ESRGAN baseline.

### 9. Evaluation Metrics

The primary evaluation metrics will be:

- PSNR (Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio)
- SSIM (Structural Similarity Index)

Both quantitative metrics and visual comparisons will be used.
